---
/**
 * Hero — first viewport.
 *
 * The artwork is a looping video (public/media/hero-video.*), not a still. It
 * replaces the pre-composited canopy/ground photo that used to sit here, so the
 * stage is landscape now rather than portrait — see .hero__stage.
 *
 * It plays itself: autoplay + muted + playsinline is the only combination every
 * browser lets start without a gesture, and the source is encoded without an
 * audio track so `muted` is a statement of fact rather than a mute button.
 * `loop` is what makes it continuous.
 *
 * Motion lives in src/scripts/hero.ts, hooked on data-hero-* attributes.
 */
interface Props {
  /** Broken by hand, not by wrapping — the three lines are the composition. */
  lines?: string[];
  primaryLabel?: string;
  primaryHref?: string;
  ctaLabel?: string;
  ctaHref?: string;
  /** The cue under the actions. */
  scrollLabel?: string;
  /** What the full-bleed frame says once the reveal has finished. Rendered
   *  with set:html so the phrase in the middle can carry weight. */
  revealLines?: string[];
  /** Extensionless base under /media — .avif and .webp are both requested. */
  image?: string;
  imageAlt?: string;
}

const {
  lines = ['We design for', 'the learning', 'economy.'],
  primaryLabel = 'Book a call',
  primaryHref = '/contact',
  ctaLabel = 'See the work',
  ctaHref = '/work',
  scrollLabel = 'Scroll down',
  /* One sentence, broken where the frame breaks it. The middle phrase is the
     one the headline above has already used, so it is the phrase that carries
     weight here too. */
  revealLines = [
    'Tribe helps the <strong>learning</strong>',
    '<strong>economy</strong> close the gap between',
    'product and brand.',
  ],
  image = '/media/hero-tree',
  imageAlt = 'A person reading under a broad tree on open grassland',
} = Astro.props;
---

<section class="hero" data-hero data-chapter-section="1" data-nav-section="Home" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
  <div class="hero__texture" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  {/* The drawn guides: two hairline verticals and one large circle. Purely
      decorative, and early in the section so they paint under the artwork and
      the copy without needing a z-index of their own. */}
  <div class="hero__guides" aria-hidden="true">
    <span class="hero__guide hero__guide--left"></span>
    <span class="hero__guide hero__guide--right"></span>
    <span class="hero__ring"></span>
    <span class="hero__ring hero__ring--inner"></span>
  </div>

  {/*
    The artwork. One photograph, two shapes: a portrait window on the left
    while the hero is at rest, and the whole viewport once the reveal has run.

    It is a still, not a film. The looping video that used to be here had the
    whole composition baked into it — the tree left, the clear ground right,
    even the white squares along the bottom — so the frame only read correctly
    at one size and the squares could not take part in anything. The still
    carries the photograph and nothing else; the window and the squares belong
    to the page, so both can move.

    Two layers, and the second one is the whole mechanism:

      .hero__media   the photograph. Full-bleed, `cover`, and NEVER touched —
                     no transform, no scale, no shift, at any point in the
                     page's life. What you see in the small frame is the same
                     pixels at the same size you will see when the frame is the
                     whole screen; nothing about the picture changes, only how
                     much of it is uncovered.

      .hero__pixels  the mask, and it is made of squares. A field of tiles in
                     the page's own ground colour covering the whole viewport,
                     with the ones over the resting frame taken out from the
                     start — so what the reader arrives on is a portrait box of
                     photograph in a field of page. Scrolling takes the rest of
                     the tiles away in random order, and that is the reveal: the
                     picture is uncovered a square at a time.

    There is deliberately no transition between a small frame and a large one.
    An earlier cut animated a `clip-path` window across the screen and scaled
    the photograph inside it, which is a different idea — the frame travelling
    and growing. This one has no travel and no growth: one photograph, fixed,
    and a mask that dissolves.
  */}
  <figure class="hero__stage" data-hero-stage>
    {/*
      Nested on purpose. .hero__stage carries the scroll-driven scale (it
      has to — pointer parallax also translates it). Hover needs its own
      scale, on its own element, or the two tweens would fight over the
      same property on the same target.
    */}
    <div class="hero__zoom" data-hero-zoom>
      <div class="hero__base" data-hero-base>
        <picture>
          <source srcset={`${image}.avif`} type="image/avif" />
          <img
            class="hero__media"
            data-hero-media
            src={`${image}.webp`}
            alt={imageAlt}
            width="2878"
            height="1926"
            decoding="async"
            fetchpriority="high"
          />
        </picture>
      </div>

      {/* Built by src/scripts/hero.ts, which needs the viewport to know how
          many. Empty here so there is nothing to clear up first, and inert:
          the squares are the artwork's edge, not a control. */}
      <div class="hero__pixels" data-hero-pixels aria-hidden="true"></div>

      {/* Two darkenings, and they are different jobs.

          The veil is a thin wash over the finished photograph — it arrives with
          the last of the tiles and stays for the hold. White type on a bright
          sky is the reason: the sentence sits over the top third of the frame,
          which is the brightest part of it.

          The fall is the way out. Black, rising from the foot of the screen
          across the hold, until the whole frame is under it — so the section
          that follows can begin on black without a cut between them. */}
      <div class="hero__veil" data-hero-veil aria-hidden="true"></div>
      <div class="hero__fall" data-hero-fall aria-hidden="true"></div>
    </div>
  </figure>

  {/* What the full frame says. In the markup rather than written in by the
      script, so it is real text in the document at every moment — the script
      only ever changes how it is drawn. Out of the accessibility tree until
      the reveal brings it in. */}
  <p class="hero__reveal" data-hero-reveal aria-hidden="true">
    {revealLines.map((line) => (
      <span class="hero__reveal-line" data-hero-reveal-line>
        <Fragment set:html={line} />
      </span>
    ))}
  </p>

  <div class="hero__grid u-container">
    <!-- The headline and who it is for at the top, the pitch and the two ways
         in at the bottom. The gap between them is the column, not a margin —
         see .hero__lede's margin-top: auto. -->
    <div class="hero__col hero__col--text">
      <span class="hero__gap hero__gap--lead" aria-hidden="true"></span>

      <div class="hero__head">
        <h1 class="hero__title" id="hero-title" data-hero-title>
          {lines.map((line) => <span class="hero__line">{line}</span>)}
        </h1>
      </div>

      {/* The spacing around and inside the copy, as boxes rather than margins.
          All three are elastic: see .hero__gap. */}
      <span class="hero__gap hero__gap--mid" aria-hidden="true"></span>

      <div class="hero__lede">
        {/* The tagline and the pitch paragraph that used to stand here are
            gone. The reference sets this column as a headline and two ways in
            and nothing else, and the sentence they carried is the one the
            full-bleed frame says a scroll later — kept once rather than
            twice. */}
        <div class="hero__actions" data-hero-aside-item>
          <a class="btn" href={primaryHref} data-magnetic="0.2">
            <span class="btn__fill" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <span class="btn__label" data-magnetic-inner>{primaryLabel}</span>
          </a>

          <a class="btn btn--ghost" href={ctaHref} data-magnetic="0.2">
            <span class="btn__fill" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <span class="btn__label" data-magnetic-inner>{ctaLabel}</span>
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>

      {/* The cue. Under the actions and quiet, because it only says what the
          page already wants — the rest of this section is a scroll away. */}
      <p class="hero__scroll" data-hero-aside-item>
        <span class="hero__scroll-mouse" aria-hidden="true">
          <span class="hero__scroll-wheel"></span>
        </span>
        <span class="hero__scroll-label">{scrollLabel}</span>
      </p>

      <span class="hero__gap hero__gap--foot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .hero {
    /* Above the section that follows it, and that is what makes the overhang
       mean anything.

       The picture runs past the foot of this section on purpose — that strip
       is where the gradient lives — but a strip hanging out of a box still
       obeys paint order, and the next section is later in the document. So
       Overclock was drawn straight over the last 64 pixels of the photograph:
       the grass, and the base of the tree. What looked like the image being
       cropped was the next section's top edge sitting on top of it.

       One line, and it only has to out-rank what comes immediately after —
       this section is scrolled off the screen long before anything else on
       the page is reached. */
    position: relative;
    /* Top of the page's paint ladder — see the note in index.astro, which sets
       the rest of it. Every chapter has to out-rank the one after it for as
       long as its own handover lasts, and document order gives the opposite;
       this one only has to out-rank Overclock, which it overhangs by the tail
       of its own gradient. */
    z-index: 4;

    /* Two rectangles, and they are different kinds of thing.

       --hero-frame-* is the RESTING FRAME: the box of photograph the reader
       arrives on. A size in pixels rather than insets into the stage, because
       it is a drawn rectangle and a drawn rectangle has a size — stated as
       fractions it changed shape at every window, which is not what a
       composition does. Only its left edge is a fraction, since where the box
       sits across the page does follow the page; it is vertically centred,
       and the copy has the right of the screen.

       --hero-subject-* is THE SUBJECT: where the tree and the person are
       inside the PHOTOGRAPH, as insets into the image. Stated against the
       picture and not the screen because it is a fact about the picture.

       The script fits the second into the first: it works out the scale and
       offset that put the subject inside the frame, and that is the
       photograph's resting transform. So the frame can move and the subject
       follows it, at any viewport, with nothing hand-matched — which is what
       "flexible" has to mean here. Neither number knows about the other; the
       arithmetic between them lives in one place, in hero.ts. */
    /* 496 x 649 at 1440 wide, and proportional to the screen from there — so
       a broader window gets a broader frame rather than the same rectangle
       stranded in the middle of more page. Written as the ratio those two
       numbers are of 1440 (34.44% and 45.07%) rather than as the pixels, so
       the pair cannot drift apart when one is edited.

       The height follows the WIDTH, not the height of the window. It is a
       drawn rectangle with a shape, and tying its two sides to two different
       axes is what makes a shape stop being one — on a short wide screen it
       would flatten. What stops it running off a short screen is the clamp in
       hero.ts, which caps it against the stage. */
    /* Fractions of the stage's WIDTH, not lengths.

       They read like percentages because that is what they are: 496 and 649
       against a 1440 screen. Stated as `vw` first and that was wrong in a way
       worth recording — a custom property is not resolved to pixels unless it
       has been registered, so `getPropertyValue` handed the script the string
       "34.444vw" and `parseFloat` took the 34 and threw the unit away. The
       frame came out 34 pixels wide, which is one tile. Plain numbers cannot
       be misread that way: the script multiplies, and there is no unit to
       lose. */
    --hero-frame-w: 0.3;
    --hero-frame-h: 0.39;
    --hero-frame-l: 0.17;
    /* How far below the middle the box sits, as a share of the stage.

       Centred exactly, a frame this size still puts its top edge close to the
       nav — and the nav is a floating pill, so "close" reads as the artwork
       running into it rather than as two things sharing a screen. Dropped, the
       gap above is clearly bigger than the pill and the box is unmistakably
       below it. The clamp in hero.ts is what stops this pushing the foot off a
       short screen. */
    --hero-frame-drop: 0.05;

    --hero-subject-l: 0.25;
    --hero-subject-r: 0.24;
    --hero-subject-t: 0.17;
    --hero-subject-b: 0.1;

    /* Air above the copy, on top of the nav's own band. Roughly a third of what
       it was: with the artwork no longer a column beside the type but the
       section's ground, this padding was the only thing holding the headline
       down, and it sat a good 50px lower than the composition wanted. */
    --hero-pad-top: clamp(0.5rem, 1.1vw, 1.5rem);

    /* Where the copy starts. The hero's own top padding restated as a value
       rather than only applied as one, so anything that has to line up with
       the copy can read the same number instead of a hand-matched copy. */
    --hero-media-top: calc(var(--nav-height) + var(--hero-pad-top));

    /* The artwork's own size and placement variables used to live here — a
       scale, a two-axis offset and a left-edge feather, all of them tuning how
       a `contain`ed video sat inside its box. Every one is gone with that
       video: the photograph is `cover`, full-bleed, and never transformed, so
       there is nothing left to place. See .hero__media. */

    /* The copy's own shift, left of where the grid puts it. Deepened from
       -28px: the artwork moved right when the resting frame did, and the
       column stayed where it was — so the gap between the two closed and the
       headline lost the air on its left that the composition is built on. */
    --hero-copy-x: -72px;
    /* Was 38px, a downward nudge against the film. It is 0 because the copy is
       centred vertically now, and a nudge is the one thing that cannot survive
       that: it moves the block off the middle by its own value and doubles
       into the difference between the space above the headline and the space
       below the buttons. Kept as a declared 0 rather than deleted — it is read
       inside a `var()` below, and an undeclared one invalidates the whole
       declaration. */
    --hero-copy-y: 0px;


    position: relative;
    /* The video's own white, so its edge does not draw a box — see
       --color-bg-hero. */
    background: var(--color-bg-hero);
    /* Exactly one viewport. The scroll scene depends on this: a scene shorter
       than the viewport makes the end of the pin unreachable. Room for the
       overlaid nav is reserved with padding instead of layout height. */
    height: 100svh;
    min-height: 34rem;
    /* The same inset top and bottom, and the symmetry is the point: the copy
       centres itself inside this box, so the box has to be centred in the
       section or the copy is not. The top inset clears the nav and the bottom
       had nothing to answer it — measured at 1440x900 that put 287px above the
       headline against 116px under the buttons, which reads as copy sitting on
       the floor rather than as a centred block.

       It costs height, and on a short screen that comes out of the elastic gaps
       rather than out of the copy: they collapse before anything is pushed
       past the fold. */
    padding-block: calc(var(--nav-height) + var(--hero-pad-top));
  }

  /* --- Drawn guides ---------------------------------------------------------
     Two verticals and a circle, all hairlines, all taken off the reference
     frame rather than placed by eye.

     The circle was solved, not guessed. Its arc crosses the top edge of the
     frame at 215 and 1065 and the bottom edge at 95 and 1185, of a 1282x743
     view — four points, which is more than enough to pin a circle down. Half
     widths of 425 at the top and 545 at the bottom put its centre below the
     middle:

       r^2 - cy^2 = 425^2     and     r^2 - (H - cy)^2 = 545^2
       -> cy = 450 (60.5% of the height), r = 619 (48.27% of the width)

     Both crossings reproduce exactly from those two numbers, so what is below
     is the frame's geometry rather than an impression of it.

     The diameter is a percentage of the width and `aspect-ratio` supplies the
     height, so the ring scales with the viewport and stays a circle rather
     than becoming an ellipse on a short window.

     The verticals measured 10.76% in on the left and 11.39% on the right — one
     symmetric inset read twice with a few pixels of error either way. 11% is
     the mean, used on both sides.

     Desktop only, at the same 1200px the two-column hero itself needs. Below
     it the section is one column with the video behind the copy, and a circle
     wider than the screen with two rules through it is noise rather than
     structure. */
  .hero__guides {
    display: none;
  }

  @media (min-width: 1200px) {
    /* Gone from the wide layout. It was a faint pixel grid masked to the
       right-hand side, and with the drawn guides above now carrying that side
       of the composition the two were competing for the same empty space. It
       stays below 1200px, where there are no guides. */
    .hero__texture {
      display: none;
    }

    .hero__guides {
      position: absolute;
      inset: 0;
      display: block;
      overflow: hidden;
      pointer-events: none;
      /* How far in the two verticals sit. Named because the inner circle is
         derived from it — see .hero__ring--inner — so the two cannot drift
         apart if this is ever retuned. */
      --hero-guide-inset: 11%;
      /* Over the artwork, under the copy. The stage is z-index 0 and these
         come before it in the document, so with no z-index of their own they
         painted underneath and the video's frame cut both circles wherever it
         overlapped them. 1 puts them across the film; the copy column is 2 and
         still sits on top of everything. They take no pointer events, so
         nothing about what is clickable changes. */
      z-index: 1;
    }

    .hero__guide,
    .hero__ring {
      position: absolute;
      /* Faint enough to read as paper rather than as drawn lines, and a fifth
         stronger than the 0.07 it started at — at that value the arcs were
         only really there on a good screen. */
      border-color: rgb(10 10 10 / 0.085);
      border-style: solid;
    }

    .hero__guide {
      top: 0;
      bottom: 0;
      border-width: 0 0 0 1px;
    }

    .hero__guide--left {
      left: var(--hero-guide-inset);
    }

    .hero__guide--right {
      right: var(--hero-guide-inset);
    }

    /* Two of them, concentric. The outer one is the circle solved from the
       frame; the inner is sized to meet the verticals, which is the only
       number it needs — everything else it shares. */
    .hero__ring {
      --hero-ring-d: 96.27%;

      left: 50%;
      top: 60.5%;
      width: var(--hero-ring-d);
      aspect-ratio: 1;
      translate: -50% -50%;
      border-width: 1px;
      border-radius: 50%;
    }

    /* Tangent to the two verticals rather than a fraction of the outer ring.
       Centred at 50%, a circle touches a line 11% in from the edge when its
       radius is the 39% between them — so its diameter is the width less both
       insets. Written that way rather than as the 78% it works out to, so it
       stays touching if --hero-guide-inset ever moves. */
    .hero__ring--inner {
      --hero-ring-d: calc(100% - 2 * var(--hero-guide-inset));
    }
  }

  /* Faint pixel field behind the copy, masked so it only ever sits on the
     right-hand side — the same technique as .showcase__texture and
     .two__texture, so the three read as one motif rather than three. */
  .hero__texture {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background-image:
      repeating-linear-gradient(90deg, rgb(0 0 0 / 0.018) 0 3.5rem, transparent 3.5rem 7rem),
      repeating-linear-gradient(0deg, rgb(0 0 0 / 0.018) 0 3.5rem, transparent 3.5rem 7rem);
    mask-image: radial-gradient(46% 52% at 82% 44%, #000 0%, transparent 74%);
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  /* One column, on the right. The artwork is no longer a column beside it —
     it is the section's ground — so the grid's only job is to hold the copy
     where the video leaves room for it. The empty first track is that room:
     it is what keeps the copy off the tree at every width, and it is sized in
     the same 1.55 : 1 proportion the two columns used to share. */
  .hero__grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.55fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: clamp(1.5rem, 4vw, 5.5rem);
    align-items: stretch;
    height: 100%;
  }

  .hero__col--text {
    grid-column: 2;
  }

  .hero__col {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    min-width: 0;
  }

  /* Type sits above the artwork — the canopy reaches into the copy's column. */
  .hero__col--text {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 2;
    /* Its own pair — see the note at the top of this file. Applied to the
       column and not to the grid track, so the track keeps its measure and the
       headline still breaks where it was set to.

       `left`/`top` on an already-relative box, not a transform, and that is
       load-bearing. hero.ts animates this column out of the scene with a `y`
       tween; GSAP writes that as an inline `transform`, having first read the
       CSS one and folded it in. An inline style beats the `transform: none`
       the narrow layout relies on, so the desktop offset survived every
       resize — drag a wide window down to a phone and the copy stayed 28px
       left, clipped against the gutter, until the page was reloaded. It looked
       right after a reload only because GSAP reads `none` at that width and
       writes x: 0.

       Moving it to the `translate` property does not help: GSAP nulls the
       independent transform properties on anything it animates, writing
       `translate: none; rotate: none; scale: none` alongside its own
       `transform`. `left`/`top` are the only offsets here it has no reason to
       touch, and on a relative box they move the paint without moving the
       flow — so the animation composes over them instead of replacing them. */
    left: var(--hero-copy-x);
    top: var(--hero-copy-y);
  }

  /* --- Headline -----------------------------------------------------------
     Sized so the longest line — "Credibility" — fits its column at every
     width, because it may not wrap and it may not be cut.

     Both of those are load-bearing: the three lines are broken by hand, so
     `white-space: nowrap` is what keeps the composition, and hero.ts splits
     this into masked lines for the intro, which means every line sits in an
     overflow:hidden box. A line wider than the column is therefore silently
     sliced rather than wrapped — which is what the old headline was doing to
     "for" before it was resized.

     The copy column is the 1fr of `1.55fr 1fr` inside the container, and this
     face sets "Credibility" at about 4.7 × its font size. 5vw leaves roughly
     a third of the column spare at every width, and the 6rem cap is the same
     slack once the container stops growing at 1920. */
  .hero__head {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: clamp(0.5rem, 0.9vw, 0.75rem);
  }

  .hero__title {
    /* 16px down from where this sat: 5vw put it at 72px on a 1440 screen and
       it is 56px there now. The clamp is scaled by the same ratio rather than
       having 16px taken off each end — a fixed subtraction changes the
       headline by a different proportion at every width, which is how a type
       scale stops being one. */
    font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 3.9vw, 4.6875rem);
    font-weight: 400;
    line-height: var(--leading-display);
    letter-spacing: -0.025em;
    /* None on the desktop row. --hero-pad-top above is the air over the
       headline, and a second helping here was the other half of why it sat
       low. The phone rule further down keeps its own. */
    padding-top: 0;
  }

  .hero__line {
    display: block;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  /* --- Lede ----------------------------------------------------------------
     The pitch and the two ways in, pushed to the foot of the column by the
     auto margin rather than by a fixed offset — so the space between it and
     the headline is whatever the viewport has left, at any height. */
  /* Pushed to the foot of the column by the auto margin, and then held well
     clear of it by the bottom one — which is the knob that decides how high
     this block sits, since nothing under it is fixed. It was 44px, which put
     the pitch and the buttons in the section's bottom corner with about 195px
     of empty column standing between them and the tagline. */
  .hero__lede {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: clamp(1rem, 1.6vw, 1.5rem);
  }

  /* The spacing around and inside the copy: above the headline, between it and
     the pitch, and under the buttons. Three boxes rather than three margins.

     The spacing was margins first, and went through three tries. `auto` read
     well at the height it was designed against and nowhere else — on a
     1061x1285 tablet it opened to roughly 750px, the headline at the top of the
     screen and the buttons at the bottom with nothing between them. A 29svh
     clamp fixed that end and broke the other: the scene pins this section at
     exactly one viewport, so on a short screen the buttons it pushed past the
     bottom edge were cut off rather than scrolled to. Subtracting a fixed 32rem
     from the viewport height covered the short case only as long as the copy
     was the height it was on the screen I measured — at 1263 wide the headline
     takes a third line and the same subtraction is 70px short.

     The mistake in all three is that a margin is written in advance, and how
     much room is left is not knowable in advance: it depends on where the
     headline wraps, and that depends on the width. So the space is a box now,
     and it measures itself.

     Growing into the free space takes what the column has and nothing more, so
     it cannot overflow — when the copy fills the column there is none to take
     and these collapse to nothing. Each cap is the other end, holding a gap at
     its designed size instead of letting it run on a tall screen. No viewport
     maths, and no breakpoint.

     All three are grow-only, and none has a height of its own. That part is
     deliberate: the column's grid row is content sized, so it grows with what
     is in it and there is never negative free space for flex-shrink to work
     on. A height here would be a height everywhere, overflow included — an
     earlier version gave the foot gap `height: clamp(2.5rem, 7.5vw, 7.5rem)`
     and at 1263x480 it sat at its full 95px while the buttons hung 53px past
     the bottom edge. Written as a cap on space it can only claim, it lets go
     by itself.

     Together they are also what centres the copy. The lead and foot gaps grow
     at the same rate and have no cap, so whatever is left after the middle gap
     reaches its own is split evenly above and below — the block sits in the
     middle of the column without `justify-content: center`, which centres by
     overflowing equally in both directions and would push the headline up
     under the nav on a short screen. These cannot overflow at all. */
  .hero__gap {
    flex: 1 1 0;
    min-height: 0;
  }

  /* Capped, because this one is a step inside the copy rather than air around
     it: uncapped it takes a third of the column on a tall screen and the pitch
     drifts away from the headline it belongs to. Everything it cannot use goes
     to the two outside gaps, which is what keeps the block centred rather than
     bottom heavy — the whole free space used to land here, and the copy sat on
     the floor of the section with the headline stranded at the ceiling. */
  .hero__gap--mid {
    /* The floor is this one's alone. The outside gaps are air and may go to
       nothing, but this is the space between two pieces of copy: at 1263x560
       the column ran out, all three collapsed together, and the pitch came to
       rest against the line above it. */
    min-height: 1.25rem;
    max-height: clamp(2rem, 7vh, 5.5rem);
  }

  .hero__actions {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.75rem;
  }

  /* --- Media ---------------------------------------------------------------
     The section's ground, covering the whole viewport behind everything else.
     It used to be a column in the grid, which cropped a composition cut for
     the full frame down to half the width. */
  /* Pinned to the foot of the section rather than filling it, and shorter than
     it by --hero-media-h. Two things come out of that:

     the frame gets smaller, because `contain` fits it to this box and the box
     is no longer the whole screen; and it stands *on* the section's bottom
     edge, which is what stops the grass reading as cut off. Filling the height,
     the artwork ran to the bottom of the window with nothing under it, so the
     edge of the video and the edge of the screen were the same line and the
     picture looked sliced by it rather than seated on it. */
  .hero__stage {
    position: absolute;
    /* Top levelled with the headline, foot on the section's own — so the box
       is everything below the line "We" starts on, and the frame is as large
       as that leaves room for. Its width follows, since `contain` keeps the
       ratio.

       A share of the height (78%) came first and was the wrong control: it set
       the artwork's size directly and let the top edge land wherever that
       worked out, which was a good 70px below the headline. Stated as two
       edges instead, the size is a result and the alignment is the rule. */
    /* Full height, not levelled with the headline. The inset used to start
       this box at --hero-media-top so its top edge lined up with the line "We"
       starts on. That cost the artwork size for an alignment nobody can see:
       the frame is `contain`ed and seated on the box's floor, so the empty
       band the inset created was simply video-white above the composition —
       the same colour as the section. Removing it lets `contain` fit a frame
       bound by the width instead of the height, which is the larger of the
       two. The artwork does not move; only its size changes. */
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    margin: 0;
    /* Pointer parallax is applied here. */
    will-change: transform;
  }

  /* Same box as .hero__stage (inset: 0 inside a sized, positioned parent), so
     inserting it changes nothing about where .hero__base ends up — it only
     gives the hover zoom a scale target of its own, independent from the
     scroll-driven scale on .hero__stage. */
  .hero__zoom {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    transform-origin: 50% 50%;
    will-change: transform;
  }

  /* Full width, and the picture's own height at that width — 1.494 : 1, so on
     any window wider than it is tall the box runs past the bottom of the
     screen. That overhang is the point: it is where the gradient stacks, and
     it is why nothing is cropped off the foot of the photograph. Anchored to
     the top rather than inset on all four sides, since it is the bottom that
     is meant to overflow. */
  .hero__base {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    /* Never shorter than the screen: on a narrow window the picture's own
       height at full width falls below the viewport, and a media box that
       stops short of the fold is a band of page under the artwork. There the
       `cover` on the image crops it instead, which is the right trade at a
       width where there was never room for an overhang. */
    /* Against the stage's own height — `100%` — and NOT against `100svh`.

       They are not the same number once the section is pinned: ScrollTrigger
       puts an explicit height on the element it pins, and `svh` goes on
       reporting the browser's small-viewport height. Measured, the hero was
       1000px tall while `svh` said 900, so a box sized to `svh` came out
       964 — thirty-six pixels short of the bottom of the screen, and that gap
       was a band of page under the photograph exactly where the picture was
       supposed to be running out. `100%` is whatever the section actually is,
       pinned or not. */
    height: max(100%, calc(100vw / 1.494));
  }

  /* The photograph. Full-bleed and `cover` — the size it ends at.

     It does not stay that size. The script gives it a resting transform that
     shrinks it and moves it left, so that the tree and the person land inside
     the resting frame whole; scrolling runs that transform back to nothing,
     which is the picture arriving at the size the stylesheet already gives it
     here. So `cover` is the destination, and the transform is how far from it
     the picture currently is.

     No `object-position` offset, though. Where the picture sits at rest is the
     transform's business, and two mechanisms moving the same image is how they
     end up fighting.

     `cover`, and the trade it makes is the deliberate one.

     The choice is forced by the shapes: the photograph is 1.49 wide to tall
     and a screen is wider than that, so one of the two axes has to give.
     `contain` gives the width — the picture is whole but stands in the middle
     of the page with a margin down each side, which at 1440 was about 47px and
     on a broad monitor is far more. `cover` gives the height — the picture
     runs edge to edge and loses a band off the top and the bottom.

     This is `cover` because the frame is the point: a photograph inset from
     both sides reads as a picture placed on a page, and what the reveal has
     been building to is the picture BEING the page.

     `object-position: center`, so what it loses it loses evenly. That is the
     whole of the protection here — the crop is symmetric, so the tree stays on
     its own axis and the horizon stays where the composition put it. The band
     is real and it grows with the screen's width: at 1440x900 it is about 32px
     off each end, and on a 1920x940 screen nearer 170px. */
  .hero__media {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    object-position: center;
    transform-origin: 50% 50%;
    will-change: transform;
  }

  /* --- The field -----------------------------------------------------------
     One grid over the whole stage, sized and filled by src/scripts/hero.ts.

     This is the mask, not an effect over one. Every tile is opaque at rest
     except the block of them standing over the resting frame, which is what
     makes that frame: a rectangle of photograph in a field of page colour. The
     scroll takes the rest away in random order.

     The hole is cut by the script rather than stated here, because which tiles
     fall inside it depends on how many tiles there are, which depends on the
     viewport. --hero-frame-* above is the rectangle it cuts.

     The hole's edge is deliberately ragged. A clean rectangle of missing tiles
     reads as a box that happens to be drawn in squares; squares stepping in and
     out of the edge read as the picture having been cut out of the page, which
     is the reference's own detail and the reason the field is made of elements
     at all.

     Painted in the hero's own ground, so a square over the photograph reads as
     a hole in it rather than as a white tile on top of it. */
  .hero__pixels {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(var(--px-cols, 1), var(--px-size, 40px));
    grid-auto-rows: var(--px-size, 40px);
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  /* A pixel over its grid cell, and then a little more.

     The extra pixel is the whole reason this rule is not just `100%`. A tile
     lands wherever the grid's fractional track puts it, so two neighbours meet
     on a boundary that is rarely a whole device pixel — and the browser leaves
     a hairline of whatever is behind them showing through. Across a field this
     is not one seam, it is a drawn grid over the artwork: the photograph's sky
     comes through every vertical and every horizontal.

     Overlapping by a pixel closes them. Done with the box's own size rather
     than a `box-shadow` spread or an `outline` of the same colour, both of
     which paint a second surface per tile — over a thousand of them, on every
     frame of a scrubbed reveal.

     `will-change` is deliberately absent: the tiles only ever change opacity,
     and promoting a thousand of them to their own layers costs far more than
     it saves. */
  .hero__pixels :global(span) {
    display: block;
    width: calc(100% + 1px);
    height: calc(100% + 1px);
    background: var(--color-bg-hero);
    opacity: 0;
  }

  /* --- The two darkenings -------------------------------------------------
     Both cover the stage and neither takes the pointer. Under the sentence in
     paint order — .hero__reveal carries z-index 2 — so what they darken is the
     photograph and never the words on it. */
  .hero__veil,
  .hero__fall {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 1;
    pointer-events: none;
    opacity: 0;
  }

  /* The stage stops clipping, so the strip of photograph below the fold — and
     the gradient stacked under it — are there to be scrolled to rather than
     cut off at the window's edge. */
  .hero__stage {
    overflow: visible;
  }

  /* Thin. 20% is enough to hold white type against the sky and little enough
     that the photograph is not visibly dimmed — the picture is the point of
     the reveal, and a scrim heavy enough to be comfortable is a scrim heavy
     enough to be seen. */
  .hero__veil {
    background: rgb(0 0 0 / 0.2);
    /* The photograph's height, not the stage's.

       `inset: 0` gave it the section's 900px while the picture runs to 964, so
       the last strip of photograph was the only part of it never darkened —
       and the veil's own bottom edge drew a line straight across the image
       where the two stopped agreeing. It has to cover exactly what it is
       tinting, which is the picture, so it is sized from the same expression
       .hero__base is. */
    bottom: auto;
    height: max(100%, calc(100vw / 1.494));
  }

  /* And the fall, which is not a curtain and is not animated.

     It sits BELOW the locked screen — `top: 100%` — and it is simply always
     there. While the hero is pinned it is off the bottom of the window and
     nobody sees it; when the pin lets go and the page carries the hero up, it
     comes into view the way anything below the fold does.

     That is the difference from what was here before. A gradient tweened up
     over a pinned photograph is a curtain drawn over a still frame: the
     picture does not leave, the words do not leave, and the section underneath
     never arrives. Stacked below instead, it is scenery the scroll reaches,
     and what the reader watches is the page moving.

     Transparent at its top so it starts on the photograph's own bottom edge
     with no seam, solid well before its foot so the section under it begins on
     flat black. */
  .hero__fall {
    top: 100%;
    bottom: auto;
    /* Exactly the strip of photograph that hangs below the locked screen, and
       not a pixel more. That strip is `imageHeight - stageHeight`, and the
       image's height is its own ratio against the full width — the same 1.494
       .hero__base is built from.

       Sized to the space rather than given a height of its own, because the
       two numbers have to be the same number. At 900svh tall it had 900px to
       fade across and 64px of picture to fade over: by the time the photograph
       ran out the black was about 7% of the way in, so the foot of the image
       met the section below it as a hard line. Fitted, it reaches full black
       exactly as the picture ends.

       `100%` and not `100svh` for the same reason .hero__base uses it: the
       pinned section's real height is not what `svh` reports, and a gradient
       measured against the wrong one starts in the wrong place.

       `max(0px, …)` because on a narrow window the picture is shorter than the
       screen and there is no overhang at all — a negative height is not a
       shorter gradient, it is an invalid declaration. */
    /* One pixel past the foot of the photograph, for the same reason the tiles
       overlap each other: the photograph's bottom and the next section's top
       land on a boundary that is rarely a whole device pixel, and the browser
       leaves a hairline of whatever is behind them showing through. Against
       two blacks that hairline is faint, but it is a drawn line across the
       join — which is the one place the join must not have one. Running the
       black a pixel into the section below closes it, and costs nothing:
       what it overlaps is already the same colour. */
    height: max(0px, calc(100vw / 1.494 - 100% + 1px));
    opacity: 1;
    /* Nought at the top, solid at the bottom, and nothing in between to tune.
       A midpoint stop is what let the fade finish somewhere other than where
       the picture does. */
    background: linear-gradient(to bottom, rgb(0 0 0 / 0) 0%, #000 100%);
  }

  /* --- What the full frame says -------------------------------------------
     Centred on the viewport rather than placed in the copy column: by the time
     it is on screen the photograph is the whole page and there is no column
     left to belong to. Above the artwork and below the nav.

     The blur is the entrance and it is on each line, not on the block: the
     lines arrive one after another, and a filter on the parent would sharpen
     all three at once however they were staggered. */
  .hero__reveal {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 2;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: 0.1em;
    margin: 0;
    padding-inline: var(--page-gutter, clamp(1.25rem, 5vw, 4rem));
    color: var(--color-ink-invert);
    font-size: clamp(1.375rem, 2.4vw, 2.25rem);
    font-weight: 400;
    line-height: 1.3;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    text-align: center;
    text-wrap: balance;
    /* Inert until the reveal hands it over — it is over the photograph, and a
       paragraph nobody can see should not be taking clicks off it either. */
    opacity: 0;
    visibility: hidden;
    pointer-events: none;
    /* Sky behind white type is bright at the top of the frame. A soft shadow
       rather than a scrim, so the photograph is not dimmed to carry three
       lines of text. */
    text-shadow: 0 1px 24px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.35);
  }

  .hero__reveal-line {
    display: block;
  }

  .hero__reveal :global(strong) {
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  /* --- The scroll cue ------------------------------------------------------ */
  .hero__scroll {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    /* On the picture's own bottom line.

       Taken out of the column's flow to get there: as a flowed block its
       position was whatever the blocks above it left over, which put it a
       short step under the buttons and nowhere near the artwork. The two are
       across the page from each other and the eye reads them as a pair, so
       they have to share a line.

       `--hero-frame-foot` is that line, published by hero.ts from the frame it
       has just laid out — see the note there for why it is passed rather than
       worked out again here. The fallback keeps the cue on the page in the
       moment before the script has run. */
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    bottom: var(--hero-frame-foot, 12vh);
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--color-ink-secondary);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    line-height: 1;
  }

  /* Drawn rather than an icon file: it is two rounded rectangles, and an
     asset for that is a request for two rounded rectangles. */
  .hero__scroll-mouse {
    position: relative;
    display: block;
    width: 1.125rem;
    height: 1.75rem;
    border: 1px solid currentcolor;
    border-radius: 999px;
    opacity: 0.7;
  }

  .hero__scroll-wheel {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0.3125rem;
    left: 50%;
    width: 1.5px;
    height: 0.375rem;
    margin-left: -0.75px;
    background: currentcolor;
    border-radius: 999px;
    animation: hero-scroll-wheel 1.8s var(--ease-out-expo) infinite;
  }

  @keyframes hero-scroll-wheel {
    0% {
      opacity: 0;
      transform: translateY(-0.25rem);
    }
    35% {
      opacity: 1;
    }
    70%,
    100% {
      opacity: 0;
      transform: translateY(0.45rem);
    }
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .hero__scroll-wheel {
      animation: none;
    }
  }

  /* --- Buttons ------------------------------------------------------------- */
  .btn {
    position: relative;
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    padding: 0.8125rem 1.75rem;
    border-radius: 0.5rem;
    overflow: hidden;
    background: var(--color-ink);
    color: var(--color-ink-invert);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    will-change: transform;
  }

  /* The second way in: the same button with its two grounds swapped, so the
     pair reads as one primary and one secondary rather than as two designs.
     Its hover therefore fills rather than empties — see below. */
  .btn--ghost {
    background: transparent;
    color: var(--color-ink);
    box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px var(--color-line-strong);
  }

  .btn__fill {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background: var(--color-bg-primary);
    transform: scaleY(0);
    transform-origin: bottom center;
    transition: transform 0.55s var(--ease-out-expo);
  }

  .btn--ghost .btn__fill {
    background: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .btn__label {
    position: relative;
    transition: color 0.4s var(--ease-out-expo);
  }

  .btn:hover,
  .btn:focus-visible {
    box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px var(--color-ink);
  }

  .btn:hover .btn__fill,
  .btn:focus-visible .btn__fill {
    transform: scaleY(1);
  }

  .btn:hover .btn__label,
  .btn:focus-visible .btn__label {
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  /* Inverted from the rule above, because this button starts inverted. */
  .btn--ghost:hover .btn__label,
  .btn--ghost:focus-visible .btn__label {
    color: var(--color-ink-invert);
  }

  /* --- Pre-animation state (only when JS is live) ------------------------- */
  :global(html[data-js='true']) .hero__title,
  :global(html[data-js='true']) [data-hero-aside-item] {
    opacity: 0;
  }

  /* Nothing to hold here any more. The artwork's resting state used to be a
     clip-path this rule shut on its own floor for the intro to open; the frame
     is cut out of the tile field now, and the field is built by the script — so
     before the script runs there is no field, and the photograph standing
     full-bleed for those few frames is the honest thing to show rather than a
     flash of an empty box. */

  /* --- Responsive ---------------------------------------------------------
     1199px, not 75rem: this must not overlap the (min-width: 1200px) gate that
     builds the pinned handover, or at exactly 1200px the hero would be both
     content-height and forced into a viewport-tall pinned scene. */
  /* Still exactly one viewport below the desktop row: the handover pins this
     scene at every width now, so anything past 100svh is clipped rather than
     scrolled to. The media row takes what the title and the aside leave
     (minmax(0, 1fr) — the 0 minimum is what lets it shrink below the image's
     intrinsic height), which is what makes this hold on a short phone without
     a per-device image size. */
  /* The single-column hero, and it starts at the phone breakpoint now rather
     than at 1199px.

     The premise of collapsing early was that the contained film would be a
     short band with clear page above and below it, leaving the full width free
     for the copy. That stopped being true: at 1061x1285 the band is 597 tall
     and the copy sits straight on the tree, buttons and all. The film is cut
     for two columns, so between 768 and 1199 it now keeps them — measured, the
     copy column comes out 358px wide at 1024 and 280px at 800, clearing the
     tree by 54px and 43px, with the headline fitting at both. */

  @media (max-width: 48rem) {
    .hero {
      --hero-pad-top: clamp(1rem, 3vw, 2rem);
      padding-bottom: clamp(1rem, 3vw, 2rem);
      /* The recut already insets the tree inside the 402×874 frame. Fit the
         whole frame; shrinking it further just stamps a complete picture. */
      --hero-mobile-fit: 1;
    }

    .hero__grid {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      align-content: start;
      height: auto;
      position: relative;
      z-index: 2;
    }

    .hero__col--text {
      grid-column: 1;
      left: auto;
      top: auto;
    }

    .hero__gap {
      display: none;
    }

    /* Full section. Do not put the film in a leftover row under the copy:
       that box's top edge landed on the sky, and anything peeking above it
       (the rounded crown) was sheared into a hard line, overflow:visible
       or not — parents still clip. */
    .hero__stage {
      position: absolute;
      inset: 0;
      width: 100%;
      height: auto;
      overflow: visible;
      z-index: 0;
      will-change: auto;
      container-type: size;
    }

    .hero__zoom,
    .hero__base {
      overflow: visible;
      will-change: auto;
    }

    /* Nothing to restate here. The frame is derived from the subject's place
       in the photograph, so it follows the picture to whatever shape the screen
       is without a second set of numbers to keep in step.

       The whole 402x874 mobile film block that used to be here is gone with the
       video it sized: one photograph under one field of tiles needs no second
       cut of the artwork. */

    :global(html[data-js='true']) .hero__base {
      clip-path: none;
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 48rem) {

    /* --- The copy, as two blocks ---------------------------------------
       The headline is one thing and the two ways in are another, so the step
       between those groups is bigger than the step inside either. Everything
       is already left-aligned on the section's own gutter, so only the
       vertical steps are set here.

       (This used to describe a tagline and a pitch paragraph as well. Both are
       gone from the column — see the props at the top of this file.) */
    .hero__head {
      gap: 0.25rem;
    }

    .hero__col--text {
      gap: 1rem;
    }


    /* One line, not three stacked words. The lines are spans, so inline is
       all it takes — plus a real space between them, since the markup has
       none: they were written as separate block lines.

       They keep the base rule's nowrap. Relaxing it here, which is what this
       block used to do, let each span break on its own and quietly outranked
       the nowrap on the title: an inline child's own white-space wins over
       the inherited one, so the headline went on wrapping to three lines with
       the property that was supposed to stop it sitting right above it. */
    .hero__line {
      display: inline;
    }

    /* `:not(:empty)` is load-bearing. SplitText copies this class onto the
       spans it generates when it re-splits the headline, and some come out
       empty — measured, the h1 held six .hero__line spans: an empty one,
       "We", an empty one, "Design", an empty one, "Credibility". Each empty
       span still matched :not(:last-child) and rendered a space of its own,
       and the first child of the box being one of them is what set the
       headline 6.7px in from the tagline and body under it. Only a span with
       a word in it earns a space after it. */
    .hero__line:not(:empty):not(:last-child)::after {
      content: '\00a0';
    }

    .hero__title {
      /* One line, guaranteed: nowrap decides it, and the size is what makes
         that safe — a line wider than its column is silently sliced here
         rather than wrapped, because hero.ts masks each one for the intro.

         Measured rather than guessed: "We Design Credibility" sets at 9.66
         times its font size on one line, so 7.7vw leaves 17% of the gutters
         spare at 390px and 15% at 320px.

         Measure it with a detached probe, not a Range over this element —
         SplitText replaces the contents with block-level line divs, so a
         Range returns the column's width whatever the type is doing, which
         reads as "exactly full" at every size. */
      font-size: clamp(1.375rem, 7.7vw, 2.5rem);
      white-space: nowrap;
      padding-top: 0.5rem;
    }

    /* Every pixel this block gives back goes to the artwork: the hero is
       exactly one viewport, so the film's row is whatever the copy leaves. */
    .hero__lede {
      gap: 0.75rem;
      margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
    }
  }

  /* Short phones (Safari with the chrome up — iPhone 15 lands around 664svh).
     The pad is the only room left to give the film; fitting is already handled
     by max-height above. */
  @media (max-width: 48rem) and (max-height: 720px) {
    .hero {
      --hero-pad-top: 0.5rem;
    }
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    :global(html[data-js='true']) .hero__title,
    :global(html[data-js='true']) [data-hero-aside-item] {
      opacity: 1;
    }

    :global(html[data-js='true']) .hero__base {
      clip-path: none;
    }
  }
</style>
